  "text": "**Solution 1 (answer 2) is wrong.** Since χ_l*(G) ≥ χ_l(G), and χ_l(K_4) = 4 with K_4 having treewidth 3, we get t(3) ≥ 4. The claim that t(d) = 2 conflates the list packing number with a fractional variant and offers no real proof.\n\n**Solution 2 (answer 4) has a gap.** Its greedy argument assumes that the neighbors' bijections σ_u restrict the forbidden pairs at v in a way that makes Hall's condition hold for every subset. The case analysis for |S| ≤ 3 is only sketched. More importantly, the upper bound d+1 would make t(3) = 4 exactly match the lower bound from K_4. My recollection of the literature on list packing of bounded-treewidth graphs is that the true value is larger than the chromatic-type bound: t(2) = 3, and t(3) = 5 (I'm reconstructing this from memory and could not verify it with tools here). If that is right, the greedy argument fails, so 4 is incorrect.\n\n**Solution 3 (answer 6) is also wrong, though closer in spirit.** Its Hall-condition count is loose and its lower-bound construction is not checked. The claimed bound t(d) ≤ 2d is not established, and if t(3) = 5 then 6 is too large.\n\nNone of the three final answers matches what I believe the correct value to be (5), and I have not verified that value independently.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
